    <name>
      <text>GQ - Natural log improper Integral</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Evaluate the integral \(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@fx@}\,dx\) and determine if it converges or diverges. 
<p> First this is improper integral because  [[input:ans1a]] [[validation:ans1a]][[feedback:prt1a]]</p>
<p> So we express the integral as a limit. That is  \(\displaystyle\lim_{t\to{@const1@}} \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}} {@fx@}\,dx\). </p>
<p>To evaluate the integral, we will need to apply integration by parts. Recall the formula for integration by parts \[\int u.dv=u.v-\int v.du\] </p>
<p> You need to be careful with your choices of \(u\) and \(dv\) that will simplify the integral. So the correct choices are :
<ul>
  <li>\(u\) =[[input:ans1b]] so that \(du=\)[[input:ans1c]][[validation:ans1b]][[validation:ans1c]] </li>
 <li>\(dv\) =[[input:ans1d]] so that \(v=\)[[input:ans1e]][[validation:ans1d]][[validation:ans1e]] </li>
</ul></p>
<p> Applying these substitutions in the formula for integration by parts, we obtain the antiderivative</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int {@fx@}\,dx=\) [[input:ans1f]]-\(\displaystyle \int \) [[input:ans1g]]\(dx\) [[validation:ans1f]][[validation:ans1g]]</p>
<p> and the value of the antiderivative is </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int {@fx@}\,dx=\) [[input:ans1h]][[validation:ans1h]]</p>
<p> Evaluating the antiderivative at the limits of integration gives </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@fx@}\,dx=\)[[input:ans1i]] [[validation:ans1i]][[feedback:prt1b]]</p> 
<p> Finally evaluating the limit as \(t\longrightarrow 0\) of your answer above gives the value of the integral. (Note: \( \infty\) is entered as <b>inf</b> and \( -\infty\) is entered as <b>minf</b>). </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@fx@}\,dx=\lim_{t\to{@const1@}} \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@fx@}\,dx=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>Therefore, the integral is [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><b>Worked solution</b></p>
<p>To evaluate the integral \(\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@fx@}\,dx\) and determine if it converges or diverges, we proceed as follows;</p>
<p> First this is improper integral because the limits of integration contain a point of discontinuity, the integrand is not continuous at a point in the interval of integration and the integrand is not defined a point in the interval of integration</p>
<p> So we express the integral as a limit. That is  \(\displaystyle\lim_{t\to{@const1@}} \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}} {@fx@}\,dx\). </p>
<p>To evaluate the integral, we will need to apply integration by parts. Recall the formula for integration by parts \[\int u.dv=u.v-\int v.du\] </p>
<p> You need to be careful with your choices of \(u\) and \(dv\) that will simplify the integral. So the correct choices are :
<ul>
  <li>\(u= {@ta_1b@}\) so that \(du={@ta_1c@}\)</li>
 <li>\(dv={@ta_1d@}\) so that \(v={@ta_1e@}\)</li>
</ul></p>
<p> Applying these substitutions in the formula for integration by parts, we obtain the antiderivative</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int {@fx@}\,dx={@ta_1f@}-\displaystyle \int {@ta_1g@}dx\) </p>
<p> and the value of the antiderivative is </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int {@fx@}\,dx={@ta_1h@}\)</p>
<p> Evaluating the antiderivative at the limits of integration gives </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@fx@}\,dx={@ta_1i@}\)</p> 
<p> Finally evaluating the limit as \(t\longrightarrow 0\) of your answer above gives the value of the integral. (Note: \( \infty\) is entered as <b>inf</b> and \( -\infty\) is entered as <b>minf</b>). </p>
<p>\(
  \begin{align}
\displaystyle \int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@fx@}\,dx&=\lim_{t\to{@const1@}} \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@fx@}\,dx\\
  \displaystyle\int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@expr_udv@}\,dx &=\left[{@expr_uv@} \right]_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}}+ \int_{t}^{{@upper_limit@}} {@expr_vdu@}\,dx \\
  & ={@expr_inta@} -{@expr_intb@} \\
&= {@expr_int_t@}
  \end{align}
  \)</p>
<p>We now need to apply L'Hospitals rule to evaluate the limit as \(t\to{@const1@}\):</p> 
<p> \(\displaystyle \lim_{t \to 0}\, t^2 ln(a*t)=0\)</p>
<p>Applying this rule into the integral, we get:
</p>
<p>    \(\begin{align}
  \displaystyle\int_{{@lower_limit@}}^{{@upper_limit@}}{@expr_udv@}\,dx &=\lim_{t \to 0}{@expr_int_t@}\\
       &={@ta1@}\\
    \end{align}\)</p>
<p>Therefore, the integral converges </p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
